//? Title: Hartung–Knapp inference
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Replace the normal-theory interval for the average effect with one that uses the observed residual heterogeneity and t quantiles, and check when that adjustment, or its truncated version, is needed

The usual (Wald) random-effects interval \(\hat\mu\pm z\,\mathrm{SE}\) treats \(\hat\tau^2\) as if it were the true between-study variance. With few studies \(\hat\tau^2\) is very uncertain, often zero, and the interval undercovers. Hartung and Knapp (2001) and Sidik and Jonkman (2002) proposed two changes:

- rescale the variance of \(\hat\mu\) by the generalised Q per degree of freedom, \(s^2=\sum w_i^*(y_i-\hat\mu)^2/(k-1)\);
- use \(t_{k-1}\) quantiles instead of normal ones.

The rescaling makes the interval reflect the spread actually observed around \(\hat\mu\). The \(t\) quantiles allow for the few degrees of freedom available to estimate that spread. When the studies agree more closely than their sampling variances imply, \(s^2<1\), and the Hartung–Knapp (HK) interval can be narrower than the Wald interval. The truncated ("ad hoc") variant prevents this with \(s^2\leftarrow\max(1,s^2)\) (Knapp and Hartung, 2003; Röver et al., 2015).

`random_effects_meta(..., {.inference = MetaInference::hartung_knapp})` and `hartung_knapp_truncated` apply the adjustment to the test, the confidence interval and the prediction interval. They report the degrees of freedom and the scale factor \(s^2\). The tests reproduce `metafor::rma(test = "knha")` and `rma(test = "adhoc")` with DL and REML to \(10^{-9}\) on three datasets, one of which has \(s^2<1\).

The simulation uses REML throughout, with \(\theta_i\sim N(0.3,\tau^2)\) and 4000 meta-analyses per setting. It compares the coverage and mean width of the three 95% intervals for \(\mu\) in two designs:

- *similar*: study variances drawn from \(U(0.01,0.2)\);
- *one large study*: one study with variance 0.002 and the rest with variance 0.1, a common pattern when a single large trial dominates.

```{cpp, id=hk, file="figures/hartung_knapp.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/meta_analysis.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::MetaInference;

struct Result {
    double cover[3]{}, width[3]{};
};

Result simulate(std::size_t k, double tau2, bool one_large, std::uint64_t seed) {
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(seed);
    const MetaInference inf[3]{MetaInference::wald, MetaInference::hartung_knapp,
                               MetaInference::hartung_knapp_truncated};
    Result out;
    const int reps = 4000;
    const double mu = 0.3;
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
        std::vector<double> y(k), v(k);
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
            v[i] = one_large ? (i == 0 ? 0.002 : 0.1) : 0.01 + 0.19 * rng.uniform01();
            y[i] = mu + std::sqrt(tau2) * rng.normal() + std::sqrt(v[i]) * rng.normal();
        }
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j) {
            const auto m = dm::stats::random_effects_meta(y, v, {.inference = inf[j]});
            out.cover[j] += (m.lower <= mu && mu <= m.upper) / double(reps);
            out.width[j] += (m.upper - m.lower) / reps;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

int main() {
    std::println("| Design | tau2 | k | Coverage: Wald | HK | HK truncated | "
                 "Mean width: Wald | HK | HK truncated |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::vector<double> ks, wald, hk, hkt;
    std::uint64_t seed = 1;
    for (const bool one_large : {false, true})
        for (const double tau2 : {0.0, 0.04, 0.16})
            for (const std::size_t k : {3, 5, 10, 20}) {
                const auto r = simulate(k, tau2, one_large, ++seed);
                std::println("| {} | {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | "
                             "{:.2f} | {:.2f} |",
                             one_large ? "One large study" : "Similar", tau2, k,
                             r.cover[0], r.cover[1], r.cover[2], r.width[0], r.width[1],
                             r.width[2]);
            }

    // Coverage against k for the one-large-study design with tau2 = 0.04.
    for (std::size_t k = 3; k <= 30; k += 3) {
        const auto r = simulate(k, 0.04, true, 100 + k);
        ks.push_back(double(k));
        wald.push_back(r.cover[0]), hk.push_back(r.cover[1]), hkt.push_back(r.cover[2]);
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(ks, wald, "Wald (z)", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .points(ks, wald, "", {176, 72, 43}, 4)
        .line(ks, hk, "Hartung-Knapp", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .points(ks, hk, "", {26, 90, 147}, 4)
        .line(ks, hkt, "Hartung-Knapp, truncated", {67, 140, 70}, 2)
        .points(ks, hkt, "", {67, 140, 70}, 4)
        .abline_h(0.95, {150, 150, 150})
        .y_limits(0.6, 1.02)
        .title("Coverage for mu: one large study, tau2 = 0.04")
        .x_label("Number of studies k")
        .y_label("Coverage")
        .size(640, 400);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/hartung_knapp.svg");
}
```
