// result_end

With similar study sizes the HK interval does what it promises. The Wald interval undercovers whenever \(\tau^2>0\): 87–92% with 3–10 studies. HK restores 93–95% at every \(k\) and keeps 95% when there is no heterogeneity. The price is width, which is large with very few studies because \(t_2\) and \(t_4\) quantiles are big. At \(k=3\) the HK interval is roughly twice as wide as the Wald interval, which honestly reflects how little three studies say about \(\mu\). The truncated version is conservative with few studies: 98–100% coverage at \(k=3\), and about 0.1–0.4 wider than HK. By \(k=10\)–20 all three intervals are close.

A single dominant study breaks all three methods. With one study of variance 0.002 among others of variance 0.1, and \(\tau^2=0.04\), the large study's weight makes \(\hat\mu\) close to that study's true effect \(\theta_1\). That effect deviates from \(\mu\) with variance \(\tau^2\), but the generalised Q mostly measures the scatter of the small studies, so the interval is too narrow. Coverage is 67–88% across methods at \(k\le10\), and 88–90% even at \(k=20\). The figure shows that HK improves on Wald throughout, and truncation helps further with few studies. The real problem is that one study cannot tell us how far its own true effect lies from the average. When a single study dominates, the random-effects average of \(\mu\) inherits that study's deviation, and no variance adjustment recovers it.

>> Practical guidance

- Use Hartung–Knapp inference by default for random-effects meta-analysis with fewer than about 20 studies. It costs little when heterogeneity is absent and fixes most of the Wald undercoverage when it is present.
- Use the truncated variant when \(s^2<1\) would make the HK interval narrower than the Wald interval, especially with fewer than 5 studies. Report which variant was used.
- Expect very wide intervals with 2–4 studies. That width reflects the data, and a Wald interval that looks precise is the misleading one.
- When one study dominates the weights, no interval for \(\mu\) is reliable. Report the dominant study separately, run a sensitivity analysis without it, and interpret the pooled result as close to that study's estimate.

>> References

- J. Hartung and G. Knapp (2001). A refined method for the meta-analysis of controlled clinical trials with binary outcome. *Statistics in Medicine* 20(24), 3875-3889. doi:10.1002/sim.1009
- K. Sidik and J. N. Jonkman (2002). A simple confidence interval for meta-analysis. *Statistics in Medicine* 21(21), 3153-3159. doi:10.1002/sim.1262
- G. Knapp and J. Hartung (2003). Improved tests for a random effects meta-regression with a single covariate. *Statistics in Medicine* 22(17), 2693-2710. doi:10.1002/sim.1482
- C. Röver, G. Knapp and T. Friede (2015). Hartung-Knapp-Sidik-Jonkman approach and its modification for random-effects meta-analysis with few studies. *BMC Medical Research Methodology* 15, 99. doi:10.1186/s12874-015-0091-1
- J. IntHout, J. P. A. Ioannidis and G. F. Borm (2014). The Hartung-Knapp-Sidik-Jonkman method for random effects meta-analysis is straightforward and considerably outperforms the standard DerSimonian-Laird method. *BMC Medical Research Methodology* 14, 25. doi:10.1186/1471-2288-14-25
- D. Jackson, M. Law, G. Rücker and G. Schwarzer (2017). The Hartung-Knapp modification for random-effects meta-analysis: a useful refinement but are there any residual concerns? *Statistics in Medicine* 36(25), 3923-3934. doi:10.1002/sim.7411
